      "text": "parameter S defines the radius of influence of a single data point. A small S value (approaching 0.01) means the radius is small, causing the model to construct the decision function primarily from narrow, local patterns in the feature space. This is mechanistically consistent with PFAS rejection, where the permeation of a molecule is predominantly deter - mined by its interaction with the membrane under specific conditions ( e. g., size exclusion becomes decisive when the molecular radius exceeds the pore size). This suggested that key feature variables, which deter - mine whether PFAS molecules can pass through the membrane, may predominantly influence the PFAS separation process. Subsequent analysis using the SHAP algorithm can further pinpoint the key inde - pendent variables affecting membrane rejection. The individual contributions of SSA optimization and AdaBoost ensemble were systematically evaluated through ablation experiments in this study. As shown in Fig. 4 , the baseline KELM model achieved R 2 of 0.56 (RMSE = 10.31) on the test set, indicating limited generalization capability. Subsequent optimization with SSA significantly improved R 2 to 0.71 for the test set, representing an obvious enhancement over the baseline model. The integration of AdaBoost ensemble further elevated the model performance, achieving notably improved test performance (R 2 = 0.85, RMSE = 5.04)."
