      "section": "results",
      "text": "PFAS removal using NF and RO membranes is intricately influenced by several factors. These include the physiochemical properties of PFAS, the inherent characteristics of the membrane, and the influent water conditions. Firstly, the structural characteristics of PFAS play a signifi - cant role in the removal process. PFAS typically consist of perfluorinated alkyl chains, with molecular weights ranging from several hundred to several thousand, depending on their chemical structure and chain length. Consequently, the removal efficiency through membrane sepa - ration processes varies significantly, generally exhibiting higher removal rates for high-molecular-weight PFAS [18 , 19] . Secondly, the inherent characteristics of the membrane, such as pore size and surface properties of the membrane material, also have a profound impact on the removal efficiency of PFAS. In addition to the pore size of the membrane affecting the size-exclusion effect on PFAS, the hydro - philicity/hydrophobicity of the membrane surface and the surface charge also influence the interaction between the membrane and PFAS molecules, thereby affecting the final rejection rate [20 \u2013 22] . This effect is particularly pronounced in the process of NF separation processes of PFAS. Finally, the influent conditions, including the concentration of PFAS, pH value, concentration of inorganic ions, and operation pressure, among others, directly influence the membrane performance and PFAS removal efficiency."
